# netsurf: HTML's timer nesting clamp; the main loop wakes for the next
# timer (patch 281)
# Generated 2026-10-13
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 280)
#
# hCaptcha's hsw times the browser (docs/wasm-hcaptcha-measurement.md,
# "Timing"); measuring the clocks and the event loop the same way in both
# engines found one standards deviation and one inaccuracy:
#
#  - HTML: "if nesting level is greater than 5, and timeout is less than 4,
#    then set timeout to 4". setTimeout(fn, 0) chained ten deep ran every
#    link at 0 ms here; Chromium 0 ... 0, then 4 ms from the seventh.
#    macsurf_qjs.c: each timer keeps its nesting level (the running timer
#    task's + 1, 0 outside one; an interval's rises each run), callbacks run
#    at their level, and a timer set above level 5 waits at least 4 ms.
#    requestAnimationFrame's timer (__msRafTimeout) is not a timer task, as
#    in browsers: a frame callback's setTimeout(0) is not clamped.
#  - the frontend's idle loop slept a whole frame (16 ms) between passes, so
#    a timer was due up to 16 ms late (setTimeout(50) fired at 64 ms; the
#    new 4 ms clamp would have been 16). frontends/vita/main.c sleeps until
#    the next JavaScript timer is due when that is sooner than a frame
#    (macsurf_qjs_next_timer_ms; at least 1 ms): setTimeout(50) fires at
#    50 ms, a clamped link after 4.
#  - while a page paints the loop does not idle, so the event-loop pump
#    (qjs_pump_heap_due) now also waits for a timer due within 4.5 ms,
#    inside its existing 10 ms budget (macsurf_platform_sleep_ms, a new
#    macos9.h hook: sceKernelDelayThread). Without it the first gate run
#    failed JS-frame-local's 300-link setTimeout(fn, 1) chain (each clamped
#    link waited a frame); now the chain takes 1.20 s, Chromium 1.21 s.
#  - a page has not settled (frontends/vita/main.c page_settled, the
#    autotest's "done" -- and so its dump) while a script runs a chain of
#    short timers (macsurf_qjs_timer_chain_active: a live timer set with at
#    most 4 ms and due within 4.5 ms; animation intervals of a frame or
#    more do not count) -- for at most VITA_SETTLE_CHAIN_MS (4 s) into the
#    page, since a setTimeout(fn, 0) heartbeat never ends (the stall gate
#    pages have one; unbounded, they waited out the 20 s timeout). The
#    second gate run read JS-frame-local while its chain, now 1.2 s as in
#    Chromium, was still running.
#
# Chromium given this engine's timer lateness, or its exact-tenth clock
# values, or 25x / 50x CPU throttling, still passes hCaptcha; the demo
# still refuses the engine -- this is a standards / accuracy fix.
#
# Gate: testpages/pages/JS-timer-nesting.html (TIMERNEST 4/4, Chromium 4/4;
# the build before fails 1-2).
